# Ground truth for `precision_and_recall`, hand-labelled over the six files listed below.
#
# Seeded from the tool's own output under `corpus.glossary.nix`, then read line by line: eight
# false positives struck, six misses added. A row is one span a reader applying ASD-STE100 with
# that glossary would mark, under that rule name. Two rules may own one span when both statements
# are true of it (`returning` is an -ing verb and `return` is an unapproved word).
#
# Struck as tagger artifacts, not writing faults: `Flag`/`Default`/`Error` read as verbs in a
# table header and in `Error/Panic`, `use` in `macros use inline` (the noun-run rule), `manual` in
# `manual fixing`, `Example` in `Example config:` (the imperative rule), and `fixing` itself — a
# gerund after a noun is a technical noun, which STE permits.
#
# Added: `qualified` and `manages` (halves of a hyphenated compound, which the immunize rule
# silences), `syncs`/`Syncs` (a 3rd-person form whose base the lemmatizer cannot reach), and two
# passives with an adverb between the auxiliary and the participle, which `aux_pairs` does not
# pair.
#
# `tedi__usage.md` is entirely a code fence and carries no rows. It is in the subset so that a
# rule that starts firing inside code shows up as a precision loss.
#
# file	byte_start	byte_end	rule
